known-red: "v2 — `new Array[T](n)` still builds a ONE-element array on this lane, so a(2) dies with `2 is out of bounds (min 0, max 0)`. BOTH v2 fronts erase the keyword before anything can tell an allocation from a factory call: legacy at v2/lib/ssc1-front.ssc0 (`new Foo(x) == Foo(x)`) and F, the DEFAULT, at specs/v2.2-p6.5-fsub.ssc parseIdent (`eraseP(parseAtom(...))`). Fixing one is a half-fix. Declared rather than hidden because the other three lanes are GREEN as of this commit and that is what this case now protects; v2 is v3/SPRINT.md SSC3-1's remaining slice. Generic constructors themselves already pass here — v2 erasing `new` happens to be right for a case class — so this declaration covers the ALLOCATION only. EXPIRES by itself: the suite fails if v2 starts passing."
---

# `new C[T](args)` and `new Array[T](n)`

Root `BUGS.md` `new-array-n-builds-a-one-element-array`. Two defects that look like one, both
found while scoping `v3` (`v3/SPRINT.md` `SSC3-1`), because an SSC IR frame is a fixed-size mutable
indexed vector and `Array` is the floor a register machine stands on.

**The constructor NAME is lost when the type is generic.** The int and js lanes extract it with
`tpe match { case Type.Name(n) => n; case _ => "?" }`, and `C[T]` is a `Type.Apply`, so it falls
through to `"?"` — for *any* generic class, not only `Array`. The int lane then reports
`Unknown constructor: ?` and the js lane emits the literal `?(3)` into its output. `JsGen` already
carried the correct peel twice and this broken one-liner a third time.

**`new Array[T](n)` allocates n slots; it is not `Array(n)`.** v2 drops `new` outright
(`v2/lib/ssc1-front.ssc0`, "new Foo(x) == Foo(x)"), which is right for a case class and wrong here:
it built a ONE-element array holding `n`, so `a.length` was `1` and any store past index 0 died.
`./bin/ssc run` **exited 0** on that wrong answer, so no exit-code check could see it.

The jvm lane was already correct on both counts — it delegates to real Scala — which makes it the
reference rather than a fourth thing to fix.

Every assertion is paired with a CONTROL that already passed, so the case cannot go green by
arrays or constructors breaking wholesale, and cannot go red for one reason while hiding another:
a non-generic constructor beside the generic one, and the two working `Array` factories beside the
allocating form. The length is also taken from a COMPUTED `n`, so the arm cannot pass by folding a
literal.

```scalascript
case class Box[T](v: T)
case class Pt(x: Int)

def main(): Unit =
  println(new Box[Int](7).v)
  println(new Pt(5).x)

  val a = new Array[Int](3)
  println(a.length)
  println(a(0))
  a(2) = 20
  println(a(2))

  val n = 1 + 2
  println(new Array[Int](n).length)

  println(Array(1, 2, 3).length)
  val f = Array.fill(3)(7)
  println(f(2))
```
